# Nessus Scan Notes
## Setup, Configuration & Observations

*Personal notes from running the scans — what I did, what worked, what didn't*

---

## Installation

Getting Nessus running on Kali was straightforward compared to the OpenVAS route I tried first. OpenVAS hit a PostgreSQL version conflict — Kali ships PostgreSQL 18 and GVM expects 17, and the dependency chain to fix that got messy. Switched to Nessus Essentials which installed cleanly.

```bash
# Install
sudo dpkg -i Nessus-10.8.3-debian10_amd64.deb

# Start service
sudo systemctl start nessusd

# Access at
https://127.0.0.1:8834
```

Registration required a free Tenable account for the activation code. Free tier covers up to 16 IPs which is more than enough for a home lab.

On first launch Nessus downloads its plugin database — took around 15 minutes. After that the interface was ready.

