## Scan Configuration

**Scan type:** Basic Network Scan
**Severity base:** CVSS v3.0
**Scanner:** Local Scanner
**Authentication:** None (unauthenticated scan)

I ran both scans without credentials intentionally. An unauthenticated scan represents what an attacker with network access but no credentials would see. Authenticated scans go deeper — they log into the system and check installed software versions, registry settings, patch levels — but I wanted to simulate the external attacker perspective first.

The difference matters: an unauthenticated scan found 24 vulnerabilities on the Windows machine. An authenticated scan of the same machine would likely find significantly more because Nessus could see inside the OS.

