## Windows Workstation Scan (192.168.100.4)

Started: May 21 at 11:35 PM
Completed: May 21 at 11:53 PM
Duration: 18 minutes

The scan ran smoothly. Nessus identified the OS as Windows 11 from service fingerprinting which matched what the machine actually is — good sign that the detection is working correctly.

Most of the findings came back as informational — Nessus enumerating what's running rather than finding exploitable vulnerabilities. The two medium findings around Splunk were the most actionable.

One thing that stood out: Nessus flagged Splunk's self-signed SSL certificate on port 8089. This is the Splunk REST API / management port — the same port that came up in the Nmap scan in Project 2. Finding the same service from two different angles (port scanner and vulnerability scanner) reinforces the finding and makes it harder to dismiss.

The NTLMSSP finding was interesting — Windows leaking the machine name during authentication attempts is technically informational, but it's exactly the kind of data an attacker would collect during reconnaissance before moving to exploitation.

