## Metasploitable Scan (192.168.211.128)

Started: May 22 at 12:30 AM
Completed: May 22 at 12:48 AM
Duration: 18 minutes

The Metasploitable VM crashed once during setup and had to be restarted before the scan could run. Once it was stable the scan ran without issues.

The findings came in fast. Within the first few minutes the vulnerability count was climbing past 20, 30, 40. Watching the donut chart fill up with red was a genuinely useful visual — it's one thing to know Metasploitable is vulnerable, it's another to watch Nessus discover 62 vulnerabilities in 18 minutes.

The most significant moment was seeing the Bind Shell Backdoor finding come up with the output showing the `id` command result. Nessus didn't just detect a vulnerability — it connected to the backdoor and confirmed root access. That's Nessus functioning as a light-touch exploitation engine, not just a scanner. The output:

```
root@metasploitable:/# uid=0(root) gid=0(root) groups=0(root)
```

VNC default password was equally straightforward — Nessus tried "password" and got in. CVSS 10.0 is the right score for that. Full graphical desktop control with a one-word credential.

The Ghostcat finding (CVE-2020-1745) was one I recognized from prior research. An EPSS score of 0.9447 means that vulnerability is being exploited in the wild with near certainty. Seeing it appear on a real scan made the EPSS scoring system feel relevant rather than theoretical.

